[FINAL-TEXT] Comprehensive picture is complete. Let me consolidate findings:

- **switch3 startup script** explicitly documents: "Tagged VLAN 30 trunk subinterface on eth0 (was missing — translation bug. Without it, switch3's VLAN-30 access ports were L2-isolated from mswitch0's vlan30 SVI, so PC12/13/14 couldn't reach their gateway 172.16.30.254)."
- **Mechanism**: switch3 had eth2-eth5 (pc12/13/14 access ports) in `br_vlan30`, but `br_vlan30` had **no uplink** — the uplink `eth0` was only in `br_vlan1` raw (untagged), not as a VLAN 30 tagged subinterface
- **Fix applied**: `eth0.30` (VLAN 30 tagged subinterface of eth0) now bridges into `br_vlan30`, creating the tagged trunk path to mswitch0's `vlan30@br0` SVI
- **Current state confirmed working**: pc5→pc12/13/14 pings all succeed after fix

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch3
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on switch3, the access layer switch serving the VLAN 30 design floor (172.16.30.0/24). Switch3 implements VLAN 30 isolation using two Linux bridges: `br_vlan30` (for access ports eth2–eth5 serving pc12/13/14) and `br_vlan1` (for the uplink eth0 to mswitch0 and other default ports). The critical mistake was that the physical uplink interface `eth0` was placed only into `br_vlan1` as an untagged member — there was no VLAN 30 tagged subinterface (`eth0.30`) bridged into `br_vlan30`. Without `eth0.30`, the VLAN 30 bridge had access ports on the downstream side but absolutely no path to the upstream multilayer switch mswitch0. Every frame from pc12, pc13, or pc14 destined for their default gateway (172.16.30.254 on mswitch0's `vlan30@br0` SVI) stayed trapped inside `br_vlan30` and was flooded back out the access ports — never reaching mswitch0. As a result, no ARP could complete for the gateway, no default route was usable, and inter-VLAN routing from VLAN 10 (pc5's floor) to VLAN 30 was completely broken, producing the "STOPPED EXISTING" timeout behavior reported. Switch1 and switch2 correctly implement the same pattern with `eth0.10` / `eth0.20` tagged subinterfaces in their respective VLAN bridges. The root cause is a translation bug when converting the Cisco trunk configuration for switch3 to Linux: the `eth0.30` subinterface was omitted. The corrective fix is: `ip link add link eth0 name eth0.30 type vlan id 30; ip link set eth0.30 up; ip link set eth0.30 master br_vlan30` — this has been applied, restoring full VLAN 10 ↔ VLAN 30 reachability.
=== END ===